! COMPARE (K) AND A NONZERO OFFSET -- Reference Manual printed p.65:
!
!   "The contents of the accumulator LEFT OF THE SPECIFIED OFFSET are
!   algebraically compared with the addressed operand.  THE BITS TO
!   THE RIGHT OF THE OFFSET DO NOT PARTICIPATE IN THE COMPARISON.
!   The comparison result indicators are set to describe the
!   accumulator field as compared with the storage field.  Neither
!   operand is changed by the operation.  The arithmetic result
!   indicators are not affected."
!
! The model shifted the OPERAND left by the offset and compared it
! against the whole accumulator.  That is the same answer whenever the
! accumulator's low `offset` bits are zero -- and a different one when
! they are not, because those bits then break a tie that the manual
! says they must not take part in.
!
! BLOCK 1 IS THE WHOLE POINT and the other three are there to show how
! narrow the difference is.  The accumulator holds 0x101 and the
! operand is an eight-bit 1 at offset 8, so the accumulator field is
! 0x101 >> 8 = 1 and the comparison is 1 against 1: EQUAL.  Shifting
! the operand instead compares 0x101 against 0x100 and reports the
! accumulator HIGH, on the strength of a low byte the page excludes.
!
! Blocks 2, 3 and 4 are unchanged by the correction, which is why
! several hundred listing-verified sections of this suite pass either
! way: at offset 0, or when the high parts already differ, the two
! readings agree.  The case was untested, not wrong.
!
! HOW EACH BLOCK REPORTS.  Z,$IND clears the indicator register, the
! operand is loaded, the K runs, and the register is loaded and stored
! whole.  Each block's LOAD leaves its own RGZ standing -- p.65 says
! the arithmetic result indicators are not affected by a comparison --
! so the expected values carry RGZ = 0x20 alongside the comparison
! bit.  AL = 0x08, AE = 0x04, AH = 0x02.
!
!   T1  accumulator 0x101, operand 1, offset 8.      RGZ+AE = 0x24
!       **The low byte must not break the tie.**
!   T2  the same comparison at offset 0.             RGZ+AE = 0x24
!   T3  accumulator 0x301 -- the high part really is higher.
!                                                    RGZ+AH = 0x22
!   T4  accumulator 0x0FF -- the high part is zero.  RGZ+AL = 0x28
!
! ALSO ON p.65 AND NOT TESTED HERE: "If the field length and offset
! are such that high-order positions of the storage field compare with
! positions to the left of the left end of the accumulator, the
! accumulator field is extended by adding zeros to its left before the
! comparison is made", and "Neither the lost carry nor the partial
! field indicator can be turned on by a comparison operation".  The
! first is automatic in a 128-bit magnitude; the second is an absence
! this deck does not probe.

! THE INJECTION IS THIS DECK'S OWN HISTORY, which is the strongest
! form available: it was written to FAIL against the model as it
! stood, and it did -- T1 = 0x22, the accumulator reported HIGH on
! the strength of a low byte, with T2, T3 and T4 passing.  The fix
! then turned T1 to 0x24 and moved nothing else.  Re-injecting means
! restoring the old line in StretchExecute's K arm:
!     SM128 opndSh = SM128 { sign: opnd.sign, mag: opnd.mag << offSh };
!     c = smCompare(acc, opndSh);

        SLC, (8)2000.0

! -- 1. the low byte must not break the tie ---------------------
START   Z, $IND
        L(BU,64,8), A1, 0
        K(BU,8), FONE+.56, 8
        L(BU,64,8), $IND, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T1, 0

! -- 2. the same comparison at offset 0 -------------------------
        Z, $IND
        L(BU,64,8), A2, 0
        K(BU,8), FFIVE+.56, 0
        L(BU,64,8), $IND, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T2, 0

! -- 3. the high part really is higher --------------------------
        Z, $IND
        L(BU,64,8), A3, 0
        K(BU,8), FONE+.56, 8
        L(BU,64,8), $IND, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T3, 0

! -- 4. the high part is zero -----------------------------------
        Z, $IND
        L(BU,64,8), A4, 0
        K(BU,8), FONE+.56, 8
        L(BU,64,8), $IND, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T4, 0

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this every 64-bit
! field below would straddle two words and be refused.
        SLC, (8)2050.0

FONE    DD(BU,64,8), 1
FFIVE   DD(BU,64,8), 5
A1      DD(BU,64,8), (8)401
A2      DD(BU,64,8), 5
A3      DD(BU,64,8), (8)1401
A4      DD(BU,64,8), (8)377

T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T3      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T4      DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
